From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 20:26:47 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: xrx500-cbm: bind the conduit the device tree describes

The device tree now describes a conduit as a child of the buffer
manager, carrying only the register value that names the switch macro it
serves and a phandle to each of its two DMA controllers. The manager's
windows and segment pools are on the parent.

Take all of it from the shape that ships:

  - the windows and the pools come off the parent node, so the phandle
    back to the manager goes away with the node that carried it;
  - the switch macro index selects the egress resources, which retires
    the module parameter that used to name a datapath port by hand;
  - the receive controller comes from its own phandle rather than from a
    constant naming one controller, so the channel handle is composed at
    probe from the controller the device tree names.

With one conduit per switch macro the driver stops being a singleton.
The two locks that serialise the enqueue windows and the traffic
manager's staging window become the driver's, because each of those is
one piece of hardware shared by every conduit, and the parameter
callbacks walk a list of bound conduits instead of following a pointer
to the only one. Receive ownership defaults on: nothing else consumes a
macro's receive ring in this shape.

The second macro's row is carried from the vendor's dequeue table rather
than derived: its dequeue port is drained by a different DMA controller
from every front-panel port of the first macro, not merely a different
channel, and the arithmetic that fits the first macro's six consecutive
rows gives the wrong answer for it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

